\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Class Epstein zetas sum to a Dirichlet value}
\date{2026-11-02}

\p{The preceding section identifies a local factor with the
[discriminant character](fdip-00A5). The same character has a global
sum over classes of binary forms. Let #{D<0} be a fundamental
discriminant: either #{D\equiv 1\pmod 4} and #{D} is squarefree, or
#{D=4m} with #{m} squarefree and #{m\equiv 2\pmod 4} or
#{m\equiv 3\pmod 4}. A positive definite binary form of discriminant
#{D} is #{Q(x,y)=ax^2+bxy+cy^2} with #{a>0} and #{b^2-4ac=D}. Two such
forms are equivalent when one is the other after an
#{\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb Z)} change of variables. Equivalent forms
represent the same integers equally often, because the change of
variables is a bijection of #{\mathbb Z^2}.}

\p{For #{\operatorname{Re}s>1} the Epstein zeta of #{Q} is
##{E_Q(s)=\sum_{(x,y)\neq(0,0)}Q(x,y)^{-s}.}
Let #{\chi_D} be the \vocabk{quadratic center character}{fdip-0049} of #{D}.
It is completely multiplicative, it vanishes when #{\gcd(n,|D|)>1},
and on an odd prime it is the Legendre symbol of
\ref{fdip-00A5}. The prime #{2} is not given that reading.
When #{D} is even, #{\chi_D(2)=0} because
#{\gcd(2,|D|)>1}, which is the vanishing clause of the center note.
When #{D} is an odd fundamental discriminant, the value already stated
in \ref{fdip-0044} is
#{\chi_D(2)=(-1)^{(D^2-1)/8}}, from
\citet{Corollary 3.1, p. 4}{conradQuadraticCharacters}.
That sign is #{1} when #{D\equiv 1\pmod 8} and #{-1} when
#{D\equiv 5\pmod 8}. This is the value of the symbol in the Euler
factor #{(1-\chi_D(2)\,2^{-s})^{-1}} of the
\vocabk{center note}{fdip-0049}. Let #{w_D} be the number of
integer solutions of #{t^2-Du^2=4}. Write #{\zeta(s)=\sum_{n\ge 1}n^{-s}}
and write #{L(s,\chi_D)} for the Euler product in the center note.
Let #{\mathcal F} contain one form from each equivalence class.}

\p{Then
##{\sum_{Q\in\mathcal F}E_Q(s)=w_D\,\zeta(s)\,L(s,\chi_D).}
The weight is #{w_D=6} when #{D=-3}, #{w_D=4} when #{D=-4}, and
#{w_D=2} otherwise. For #{D=-4} the only class is #{x^2+y^2}, so that
one zeta equals #{4\zeta(s)L(s,\chi_{-4})}.}

\proof{
\p{Since #{D<0}, the equation #{t^2+|D|u^2=4} forces #{|u|\le 1}.
The solutions with #{u=0} are #{(t,u)=(\pm 2,0)}. When #{|u|=1},
#{t^2=4-|D|}, so #{t=\pm 1} when #{|D|=3} and #{t=0} when
#{|D|=4}. Thus #{|D|=3} gives 6 solutions, #{|D|=4} gives 4,
and every larger value gives two. These are the three values of
#{w_D}.}

\p{Let #{B_Q} be the full polar form
#{B_Q(v,w)=Q(v+w)-Q(v)-Q(w)}. In the coordinate basis its Gram matrix is
##{\begin{pmatrix}2a&b\\b&2c\end{pmatrix},}
and #{D=-\det(B_Q)}. The argument does not divide the middle coefficient
by #{2}. When #{D\equiv 1\pmod 4} that coefficient is odd, so the halved
matrix is not an integral Gram matrix. An element
#{\gamma\in\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb Z)} replaces that matrix by a congruent
matrix, so it preserves #{D}. Let #{\tau=(-b+\sqrt{D})/(2a)} be the
root in the upper half plane, taking the square root of positive
imaginary part. The factorization
#{Q(x,y)=a(x-\tau y)(x-\overline{\tau} y)}
shows that an automorph permutes the two roots. Determinant #{1}
preserves the upper half plane, so the automorph fixes #{\tau}. If
#{\gamma=\begin{pmatrix}p&q\\r&s\end{pmatrix}}, the fixed-point equation
is #{r\tau^2+(s-p)\tau-q=0}. The minimal equation
#{a\tau^2+b\tau+c=0} is irreducible over #{\mathbb Q}, so there is a
rational scalar #{u} with #{r=au}, #{s-p=bu}, and #{q=-cu}.
First, #{\gcd(a,b,c)=1}. A prime dividing all three coefficients
square-divides #{D}. The odd part of a fundamental discriminant is
squarefree, so that prime is not odd. If the prime is #{2}, then #{D=4m}. Writing the coefficients as
twice integers #{a'}, #{b'}, and #{c'} gives
#{m=b'^2-4a'c'\equiv b'^2\pmod 4}, and a square modulo #{4} is #{0} or
#{1}, contrary to the fundamental condition on #{m}.
Bézout's identity therefore writes #{1} as an integer combination of
#{a}, #{b}, and #{c}, and the rational scalar #{u} is an integer.
Set #{t=p+s}. The determinant #{ps-qr=1} becomes
#{(t^2-Du^2)/4=1}. Conversely each solution #{(t,u)} determines
##{p=\frac{t-bu}{2},\qquad
s=\frac{t+bu}{2},\qquad q=-cu,\qquad r=au.}
The same parity of #{t} and #{bu} makes #{p} and #{s} integers, since
#{t^2\equiv Du^2\pmod 2} and #{D\equiv b^2\pmod 2}. The form produced
by #{\gamma} has the same root and the same discriminant, so it is a
positive multiple of #{Q}. The discriminant scales by the square of
that multiple, and the multiple is positive, so the multiple is #{1}.
Thus these #{w_D} matrices are exactly the automorphs of every form of
discriminant #{D}.}

\p{Let #{R(n)} be the number of pairs #{\bigl(Q,(x,y)\bigr)} with
#{Q\in\mathcal F} and #{Q(x,y)=n}. Let #{R^\ast(n)} count only the pairs
with #{\gcd(x,y)=1}. A nonzero vector has content #{d=\gcd(x,y)}, and
#{Q(dv)=d^2Q(v)}, so
##{R(n)=\sum_{d^2\mid n}R^\ast(n/d^2).}
The stabilizer in the automorph group of any primitive vector is
trivial: an automorph fixing a vector has eigenvalue #{1}, hence trace
#{2} because the determinant is #{1}, hence #{t=2} and #{u=0}. The
group therefore acts freely on the proper representations of each
form. It remains to show that the orbits are indexed by the residues
#{b} modulo #{2n} with #{b^2\equiv D\pmod{4n}}.}

\p{Given a proper representation of #{n} by a vector #{v}, complete
#{v} to a basis #{(v,w)} of determinant #{1}. In that basis the form is
#{nx^2+Bxy+Cy^2} with #{B^2-4nC=D}. Replacing #{w} by #{w+tv}
sends #{xv+yw} to #{(x+ty)v+yw}. The term #{n(x+ty)^2} contributes
the extra middle term #{2nt\,xy}, so the middle coefficient changes
by #{2nt} and #{B} modulo #{2n} is invariant. An automorph changes the vector inside one
orbit and does not change this residue. In the other direction, a
residue #{b} produces the positive definite form
#{nx^2+bxy+cy^2} with #{c=(b^2-D)/(4n)}. That form has discriminant
#{D}, so it is equivalent to exactly one representative in
#{\mathcal F}. Transporting #{(1,0)} by such an equivalence yields a
proper representation with that residue. If two proper representations
have the same residue #{b}, replace each second basis vector by a
shear so that the middle coefficient equals #{b}. The discriminant
identity then forces both coordinate forms to equal
#{nx^2+bxy+cy^2} with the same #{c=(b^2-D)/(4n)}. Those two
determinant-#{1} bases therefore belong to the same class
representative. If #{M_1} and #{M_2} are the basis matrices,
#{M_1M_2^{-1}} carries one represented vector to the other and
preserves the form, since both bases pull it back to that coordinate
polynomial. The number of such residues is therefore the number of
orbits, and #{R^\ast(n)=w_D\rho(n)}, where #{\rho(n)} denotes that
number of residues.}

\p{The count #{\rho} is multiplicative on coprime arguments. If #{n_2}
is odd and coprime to #{n_1}, a residue modulo #{2n_1n_2} is a pair of
a residue modulo #{2n_1} and a residue modulo #{n_2}. The square
condition splits, and for odd #{n} the count #{\rho(n)} equals the
number of solutions of #{v^2\equiv D\pmod n}. Indeed #{\gcd(4,n)=1}, so
the condition modulo #{4n} splits into conditions modulo #{4} and
modulo #{n}, and exactly one of the two lifts modulo #{2n} has the
parity required by #{D} modulo #{4}. Thus #{\rho(n_1n_2)=\rho(n_1)\rho(n_2)}.}

\p{For an odd prime #{p} the same count is the number of roots of
#{v^2-D} modulo #{p^k}. If #{p} does not divide #{D} and
#{\chi_D(p)=1}, there are two simple roots modulo #{p}, and the
derivative #{2v} is invertible at each, so each lifts uniquely and
#{\rho(p^k)=2}. If #{\chi_D(p)=-1}, there is no root and
#{\rho(p^k)=0}. If #{p} divides #{D}, then #{p} divides #{D} exactly
once. Modulo #{p} the only root is #{0}, so #{\rho(p)=1}. Modulo
#{p^k} with #{k\ge 2} the valuation #{v_p(D)=1} is odd, while a square
has even valuation, so #{\rho(p^k)=0}.}

\p{At the prime #{2}, first take #{n=1}: exactly one of #{b=0,1} satisfies
#{b^2\equiv D\pmod 4}, so #{\rho(1)=1}. If #{D} is odd and
#{D\equiv 5\pmod 8}, then for #{a\ge 1} the modulus #{2^{a+2}} is a
multiple of #{8}, and no square is congruent to #{5} modulo #{8}, so
#{\rho(2^a)=0}. If #{D\equiv 1\pmod 8}, the two odd residues modulo
#{4} satisfy #{b^2\equiv D\pmod 8}, so #{\rho(2)=2}. Every later
solution is odd, because #{D} is odd. Suppose #{a\ge 1} and #{b_0} is a
residue modulo #{2^{a+1}} with #{b_0^2\equiv D\pmod{2^{a+2}}}. Its lifts
modulo #{2^{a+2}} are #{b=b_0+t2^{a+1}} for #{t\in\{0,1\}}. Expanding
gives
##{b^2=b_0^2+tb_0\,2^{a+2}+t^2 2^{2a+2}.}
This class already solves the congruence modulo #{2^{a+2}}, so the cross
term vanishes at that modulus and cannot choose #{t} there. The next
count tests #{b^2\equiv D\pmod{2^{a+3}}}. For #{a\ge 1} the quadratic
term is divisible by #{2^{a+3}}. The cross term #{2^{a+2}b_0 t} remains,
and #{b_0} odd makes exactly one value of #{t} succeed. Reduction
modulo #{2^{a+1}} sends each solution counted by #{\rho(2^{a+1})} to a
solution counted by #{\rho(2^a)}, so the two counts are equal. Thus
#{\rho(2^a)=2} for every #{a\ge 1}. If #{D} is even, inspect #{b} modulo #{4} against the condition
modulo #{8}. When #{D\equiv 4\pmod 8} the only solution is
#{b\equiv 2\pmod 4}. When #{D\equiv 0\pmod 8} the only solution is
#{b\equiv 0\pmod 4}. Thus #{\rho(2)=1}. For #{a\ge 2} write
#{D=4m}. If #{m\equiv 2\pmod 4}, then #{v_2(D)=3}. A square cannot
have that valuation, and #{3} is strictly smaller than the modulus
#{2^{a+2}}, so there is no solution. If #{m} is odd, a solution would
have valuation exactly #{2}, hence the shape #{b=2c} with #{c} odd,
and then #{c^2\equiv m\pmod 4}. An odd square is #{1} modulo #{4},
while #{m\equiv 3\pmod 4}. There is again no solution, so
#{\rho(2^a)=0} for #{a\ge 2}.}

\p{Let #{\psi(n)=\sum_{d\mid n}\chi_D(d)} and
#{g(n)=\sum_{d^2\mid n}\rho(n/d^2)}. Both functions are multiplicative, since a square divisor of a
product of coprime arguments splits, so it is enough to compare them
on prime powers. If #{\chi_D(p)=1}, then #{\rho(p^j)=2} for #{j\ge 1}.
For even #{k=2m} the sum #{g(p^k)} is #{m} terms equal to #{2} and one
term #{\rho(1)}, hence #{k+1}. For odd #{k=2m+1} it is #{m+1} terms
equal to #{2}, hence again #{k+1}. The character sum is the same
geometric sum. If #{\chi_D(p)=-1}, then #{\rho(p^j)=0} for #{j\ge 1},
so #{g(p^k)} keeps only #{\rho(1)} when #{k} is even and is #{0} when
#{k} is odd. The alternating character sum has those same two values.
If #{\chi_D(p)=0}, then #{\rho(p)=1} and #{\rho(p^j)=0} for #{j\ge 2},
so every #{g(p^k)} equals #{1}, as does #{\psi(p^k)}. Therefore
#{g=\psi}, and
##{R(n)=w_D\sum_{d\mid n}\chi_D(d).}}

\p{The identity already proved gives #{|R(n)|\le w_D\,\tau(n)}, where
#{\tau(n)} is the number of positive divisors of #{n}: the absolute
value of #{\chi_D} is at most #{1}. Since #{\tau=1\ast 1},
##{\sum_{n\ge 1}\tau(n)\,n^{-s}=\zeta(s)^2,}
which converges for #{\operatorname{Re}s>1}. Therefore
#{\sum_n R(n)\,n^{-s}} converges absolutely in that half-plane. Each
pair #{\bigl(Q,(x,y)\bigr)} with #{Q(x,y)=n} contributes one term
#{n^{-s}}, so
##{\sum_{Q\in\mathcal F}E_Q(s)
=\sum_{n\ge 1}R(n)\,n^{-s}.}
A comparison integral for one positive form does not by itself control
this sum over classes. The hypothesis #{a>0}, together with #{D<0}, is
what makes every represented value positive. The identity is not claimed
when #{D>0}: an indefinite form has an automorphism group governed by a
Pell equation, and that group need not have order #{w_D}.
The representation count and the substitution #{n=dm} give
##{w_D\sum_{d,m\ge 1}\chi_D(d)\,(dm)^{-s}
=w_D\,\zeta(s)\sum_{d\ge 1}\chi_D(d)\,d^{-s}.}
Complete multiplicativity expands each Euler factor
#{(1-\chi_D(p)p^{-s})^{-1}} as a geometric series. The product in the
[center note](fdip-0049) is therefore the Dirichlet series, which is
the identity claimed above.}

\p{For the illustration #{D=-4}, repeat the shear
#{\begin{pmatrix}1&k\\0&1\end{pmatrix}}, which replaces #{b} by
#{b+2ak} and does not change #{a}, until #{|b|\le a}. Whenever
#{a>c}, apply #{\begin{pmatrix}0&-1\\1&0\end{pmatrix}}. That matrix
exchanges the outer coefficients and changes the sign of the middle
coefficient, so it replaces #{a} by the smaller positive integer
#{c}. Each such swap decreases #{a}, and the process stops at a
representative with #{|b|\le a\le c}. Then #{b} is even,
#{ac=(b/2)^2+1}, and #{a^2\le ac\le a^2/4+1}, so #{a=1}, #{b=0},
and #{c=1}. The only class is #{x^2+y^2}, and #{w_{-4}=4}.}
}

\p{The identity concerns binary forms. The four-cycle precision has rank
#{4}. Schiemann's papers of 1990 and 1997, which have not been read
here, are not a step in the argument: this section does not claim that
one theta series separates a quaternary form from the other forms in
its genus.}

\p{\em{The Weil index remains speculative.} No product of local indices
is proved here, and Weil's paper of 1964 has not been read for this
note. The class sum does not identify an Epstein zeta with the real
normalizer.}
